dict, method, facet counts, ISO week and an HMAC-SHA256 task hash; never task text, notes, paths or the salt — to the user's own path (contributed/<hf-username>/) in the Hugging Face dataset keppy/evalroute-flywheel using the user's existing huggingface_hub login; \"--dry-run\" prints the exact rows and sends nothing; the per-install salt stays local in <HERMES_HOME>/evalroute/contribute.salt. Since evalroute 0.9.1 (schema 2) the same upload also carries lane-correction rows as lane pairs (from_lane, to_lane, method, week) with no task text, plus the max_turns value and harness name the arm ran under and, since evalroute 0.10.0, whether the arm was an explore draw and which default arm it replaced (arm ids only). Disclosure — since evalroute 0.10.0, setting evalroute.explore.fraction in config.yaml (or EVALROUTE_EXPLORE) makes that fraction of routes recommend the lane's runner-up arm from the bundled explore table instead of the default arm (and \"hermes evalroute dispatch\" then runs that runner-up model), marked on the route card; off by default. Disclosure — \"hermes evalroute install-encoder <dir|owner/name>\" (run by hand only) downloads a small text-classification model (the default id keppy/evalroute-lane-encoder is a 146 MB ModernBERT fine-tune on the Hugging Face Hub) into <HERMES_HOME>/evalroute/encoder/ and from then on \"hermes evalroute route\" runs it locally, offline, to classify the task when the keyword rules are weak, instead of the LLM call; it needs torch and transformers in the plugin environment (pip extra evalroute[encoder]), which the plugin does not install; \"install-encoder --remove\" deletes it. \"hermes evalroute export-cases\" (run by hand only) writes your --lane-pinned route rows, task text included, to a local file you name, for training your own encoder; stdout carries counts only and nothing is uploaded. As of 0.6.2 the plugin is a thin adapter: all behaviour lives in the PyPI package evalroute (>=0.10.0,<0.11), declared as a Pyth